WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.700
A dependency does not need to spread through the entire codebase.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:13.880
A narrow wrapper can centralize validation, error handling, ownership rules, resource cleanup, logging, metrics, and version-specific behavior.

3
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:22.460
This is especially valuable for parsers, cryptographic libraries, compression libraries, image libraries, networking libraries, and platform APIs.

4
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.660
Wrappers reduce coupling.

5
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:32.040
Application code can depend on a small internal interface instead of every detail of the third-party API.

6
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:39.340
If the library changes, is replaced, or requires a configuration update, the team has fewer call sites to review.

7
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:46.800
A wrapper can also hide functions that are not appropriate for the product, so engineers are guided toward the approved usage pattern.

8
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.040
Boundary validation is part of safe use.

9
00:00:50.140 --> 00:01:00.460
Inputs should be checked before calling library functions when the application can make useful decisions about size, format, path location, encoding, or policy.

10
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:07.200
Outputs and errors should be normalized so callers do not have to understand every library-specific return convention.

11
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.260
Logging should reveal useful operational context without exposing sensitive data.

12
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.420
Resource ownership should be explicit.

13
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:22.800
C++ integrations often involve handles, contexts, buffers, callbacks, and custom allocators.

14
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:28.500
The wrapper should make lifetime, cleanup, thread-safety, and exception behavior clear.

15
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:37.640
Good boundaries do not make a dependency perfect, but they make risky behavior easier to contain, test, monitor, and replace when the product evolves.
